---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const npmCode = 'npm install -g @the-agenticflow/openflows\nopenflows --version';
const breadcrumbs = [{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Getting Started' }];

const registryJson = `{
  "workspace_provider": "coder",
  "coder_module": "openflows-agent",
  "ai_gateway": {
    "primary": "coder",
    "fallback": "litellm"
  },
  "agents": {
    "nexus":    { "provider": "anthropic", "model": "claude-sonnet-4-20250514", "coder_module": "claude-code" },
    "forge":    { "provider": "anthropic", "model": "claude-sonnet-4-20250514", "coder_module": "claude-code" },
    "sentinel": { "provider": "openai",    "model": "gpt-4.1",                  "coder_module": "codex" },
    "vessel":   { "provider": "openai",    "model": "gpt-4o",                  "coder_module": "codex" },
    "lore":     { "provider": "anthropic", "model": "claude-haiku-4-20250514", "coder_module": "aider" }
  }
}`;

const PREREQS = [
  {
    name: 'Coder deployment',
    detail: 'A running Coder instance (self-hosted, VPC, or air-gapped). OpenFlows agents execute inside Coder workspaces.',
    install: 'https://coder.com/docs/install',
  },
  {
    name: 'Node.js 18+',
    detail: 'Required for the GitHub MCP server and npm install',
    install: 'https://nodejs.org',
  },
  {
    name: 'LLM access',
    detail: 'Coder AI Gateway (primary) or LiteLLM proxy (fallback) for self-hosted providers',
    install: 'Configure in Coder dashboard',
  },
  {
    name: 'Coder CLI module',
    detail: 'A Coder Registry module that installs your preferred code agent CLI into each workspace - Claude Code, Codex CLI, Aider, and more.',
    install: 'https://registry.coder.com',
  },
];

const NEXT_STEPS = [
  {
    icon: '⚙️',
    title: 'Agent Setup',
    desc: 'Tune model routing, worker slots, and per-agent prompts for Coder workspaces.',
    href: '/docs/guides/agent-setup',
  },
  {
    icon: '🧭',
    title: 'Agent Roles',
    desc: "Understand each agent's contract, recovery behavior, and workspace lifecycle.",
    href: '/docs/architecture/agent-roles',
  },
  {
    icon: '❓',
    title: 'FAQ & Troubleshooting',
    desc: 'Coder connectivity, AI Gateway routing, token scopes, and common fixes.',
    href: '/docs/faq',
  },
];

const TABS = [
  {
    label: 'npm (Recommended)',
    content: `<p>Fastest on any platform with Node.js already installed.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">npm install -g @the-agenticflow/openflows
openflows --version</code></pre>`,
  },
  {
    label: 'One-liner',
    content: `<p>Downloads the correct pre-built binary for your OS and architecture, installs to <code>~/.local/bin</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/The-AgenticFlow/OpenFlows/main/scripts/install.sh | bash</code></pre>`,
  },
  {
    label: 'Cargo',
    content: `<p>For Rust developers who prefer cargo.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cargo install openflows
openflows --version</code></pre>`,
  },
  {
    label: 'Docker',
    content: `<p>Run in a container. Mounts your config directory for persistence.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">docker run -it --rm \\
  -v "$HOME/.openflows:/home/openflows/.openflows" \\
  -e CODER_ACCESS_URL=https://coder.your-org.com \\
  -e CODER_TOKEN=your-coder-token \\
  ghcr.io/the-agenticflow/openflows:latest</code></pre>`,
  },
];

const CONFIG_TABS = [
  {
    label: 'Interactive (Recommended)',
    content: `<p>The TUI wizard asks for your Coder URL, tokens, and preferred CLI, then writes <code>.env</code> and <code>registry.json</code> automatically.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">openflows-setup</code></pre>
<p>It will walk you through:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Coder deployment URL and admin token</li>
  <li>Target GitHub repository</li>
  <li>GitHub identity - handled by Coder external auth, not pasted tokens</li>
  <li>AI Gateway endpoint (or LiteLLM fallback URL)</li>
  <li>Preferred code agent CLI (Claude Code, Codex CLI, Aider, or other Coder Registry module)</li>
  <li>Model registry for each agent</li>
</ol>`,
  },
  {
    label: 'Manual',
    content: `<p>Copy the template below into a <code>.env</code> file in your working directory. Every variable is documented inline.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Coder Platform ──────────────────────────────
# Your Coder deployment URL
CODER_ACCESS_URL=https://coder.your-org.com
# Coder API token (admin or workspaces:create scope)
CODER_TOKEN=your-coder-token

# ── Core ────────────────────────────────────────
# The repository the team will work on
GITHUB_REPOSITORY=your-org/your-repo

# Code agent CLI: "claude" for Claude Code, "codex" for Codex CLI, "aider" for Aider
# The corresponding Coder Registry module must be published in your deployment.
DEFAULT_CLI=claude

# The official GitHub MCP server. In Coder mode the token this server uses is
# injected by Coder external auth (see below) - nothing to paste here.
GITHUB_MCP_CMD=npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-github

# ── LLM Routing ─────────────────────────────────
# Primary: Coder AI Gateway (centrally managed, no keys in workspaces)
AI_GATEWAY_URL=https://coder.your-org.com/ai-gateway
# Fallback: LiteLLM proxy (for self-hosted or air-gapped providers)
# LITELLM_PROXY_URL=http://litellm:4000
# LITELLM_API_KEY=sk-litellm-key

# ── GitHub identity ──────────────────────────────
# In Coder mode, GitHub identity is handled by Coder external auth.
# Configure a GitHub external auth provider in your Coder deployment and
# include the git-config module in your workspace templates. Each agent
# then acts under its logged-in Coder user's GitHub identity - git commits,
# the GitHub MCP server, and PRs are all auditable to that identity.

# ── Debug / Logging ─────────────────────────────
RUST_LOG=info,agent_team=debug,pocketflow_core=debug</code></pre>`,
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <!-- Hero header -->
  <header class="docHero">
    <p class="docEyebrow">Quick Start Guide</p>
    <h1>Getting Started</h1>
    <p class="docLead">
      OpenFlows is the orchestration layer that runs on top of your Coder environment.
      Five agents - NEXUS, FORGE, SENTINEL, VESSEL, and LORE - collaborate to take issues
      all the way to merged, documented pull requests without human intervention. Coder
      governs <em>where</em> agents run; OpenFlows governs <em>how</em> they coordinate.
    </p>
    <div class="docMeta">
      <span class="docMetaItem">
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>
        ~10 min read
      </span>
      <span class="docMetaSep"></span>
      <span class="docMetaItem">6 steps</span>
      <span class="docMetaSep"></span>
      <span class="docMetaItem">Beginner</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="callout tip">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">What you will accomplish</p>
        <div class="calloutText">By the end of this guide you will have OpenFlows installed, configured against your Coder deployment, and running with ephemeral workspaces that turn open issues into reviewed, merged PRs.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Step 1: Prerequisites -->
  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="prerequisites">
    <span class="stepNumber">1</span>
    Prerequisites
  </h2>
  <div class="prereqGrid">
    <div class="prereqCard">
      <h3>Coder deployment</h3>
      <p>A running Coder instance (self-hosted, VPC, or air-gapped). OpenFlows agents execute inside Coder workspaces.</p>
      <a href="https://coder.com/docs/install" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get started</a>
    </div>
    <div class="prereqCard">
      <h3>Node.js 18+</h3>
      <p>Required for the GitHub MCP server and npm install</p>
      <a href="https://nodejs.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get started</a>
    </div>
    <div class="prereqCard">
      <h3>LLM access</h3>
      <p>Coder AI Gateway (primary) or LiteLLM proxy (fallback) for self-hosted providers</p>
      <p>Configure in Coder dashboard</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prereqCard">
      <h3>Coder CLI module</h3>
      <p>A Coder Registry module that installs your preferred code agent CLI into each workspace - Claude Code, Codex CLI, Aider, and more.</p>
      <a href="https://registry.coder.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get started</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <strong>Coder CLI modules - the muscle layer</strong>
    <p>FORGE edits code through a CLI code agent installed into each workspace by a <strong>Coder Registry module</strong>. The module you choose determines which CLI is available: claude-code (Anthropic's Claude Code CLI), codex (OpenAI's Codex CLI), or aider (open-source AI pair programming). Set <code>DEFAULT_CLI=claude</code>, <code>DEFAULT_CLI=codex</code>, or <code>DEFAULT_CLI=aider</code> in your environment to choose. You can also assign a different module per agent in <code>registry.json</code> (step 3) - mixing CLIs across roles is fully supported.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="install">
    <span class="stepNumber">2</span>
    Install OpenFlows
  </h2>
  <p>Choose the method that fits your setup. All methods install the same binaries: <code>openflows</code>, <code>openflows-setup</code>, <code>openflows-dashboard</code>, and <code>openflows-doctor</code>.</p>

  <div class="tabTabs">
    <button class="tabTab active" onclick="showTab(0)">npm (Recommended)</button>
    <button class="tabTab" onclick="showTab(1)">One-liner</button>
    <button class="tabTab" onclick="showTab(2)">Cargo</button>
    <button class="tabTab" onclick="showTab(3)">Docker</button>
  </div>

  <div class="tabContent active" id="tab-0">
    <p>Fastest on any platform with Node.js already installed.</p>
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader">
        <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
        <button class="copyBtn">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre><code>{npmCode}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tabContent" id="tab-1">
    <p>Downloads the correct pre-built binary for your OS and architecture, installs to <code>~/.local/bin</code>.</p>
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader">
        <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
        <button class="copyBtn">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/The-AgenticFlow/OpenFlows/main/scripts/install.sh | bash</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tabContent" id="tab-2">
    <p>For Rust developers who prefer cargo.</p>
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader">
        <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
        <button class="copyBtn">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre><code>cargo install openflows
openflows --version</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tabContent" id="tab-3">
    <p>Run in a container. Mounts your config directory for persistence.</p>
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader">
        <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
        <button class="copyBtn">Copy</button>
      </div>
      <pre><code>docker run -it --rm \
  -v "$HOME/.openflows:/home/openflows/.openflows" \
  -e CODER_ACCESS_URL=https://coder.your-org.com \
  -e CODER_TOKEN=your-coder-token \
  ghcr.io/the-agenticflow/openflows:latest</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    function showTab(index) {
      document.querySelectorAll('.tabTab').forEach((btn, i) => {
        btn.classList.toggle('active', i === index);
      });
      document.querySelectorAll('.tabContent').forEach((content, i) => {
        content.classList.toggle('active', i === index);
      });
    }
  </script>

  <p>See the <a href="/docs/getting-started/installation">Installation Guide</a> for Homebrew, source builds, and Compose setups.</p>

  <!-- Step 3: Configure -->
  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="configure">
    <span class="stepNumber">3</span>
    Configure Your Environment
  </h2>
  <p>
    OpenFlows needs a <code>.env</code> file with Coder credentials and a
    <code>registry.json</code> that maps each agent to its LLM model through the Coder AI
    Gateway. GitHub identity is handled by Coder external auth.
    You can generate both with the interactive wizard, or write them manually.
  </p>

  <div class="tabTabs">
    <button class="tabTab active" onclick="showConfigTab(0)">Interactive (Recommended)</button>
    <button class="tabTab" onclick="showConfigTab(1)">Manual</button>
  </div>

  <div class="tabContent active" id="config-tab-0">
    <p>The TUI wizard asks for your Coder URL, tokens, and preferred CLI, then writes <code>.env</code> and <code>registry.json</code> automatically.</p>
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader">
        <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code>openflows-setup</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>It will walk you through:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Coder deployment URL and admin token</li>
      <li>Target GitHub repository</li>
      <li>GitHub identity - handled by Coder external auth, not pasted tokens</li>
      <li>AI Gateway endpoint (or LiteLLM fallback URL)</li>
      <li>Preferred code agent CLI (Claude Code, Codex CLI, Aider, or other Coder Registry module)</li>
      <li>Model registry for each agent</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="tabContent" id="config-tab-1">
    <p>Copy the template below into a <code>.env</code> file in your working directory. Every variable is documented inline.</p>
    <div class="codeBlock">
      <div class="codeHeader">
        <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
      </div>
      <pre><code># ── Coder Platform ──────────────────────────────
# Your Coder deployment URL
CODER_ACCESS_URL=https://coder.your-org.com
# Coder API token (admin or workspaces:create scope)
CODER_TOKEN=your-coder-token

# ── Core ────────────────────────────────────────
# The repository the team will work on
GITHUB_REPOSITORY=your-org/your-repo

# Code agent CLI: "claude" for Claude Code, "codex" for Codex CLI, "aider" for Aider
# The corresponding Coder Registry module must be published in your deployment.
DEFAULT_CLI=claude

# The official GitHub MCP server. In Coder mode the token this server uses is
# injected by Coder external auth (see below) - nothing to paste here.
GITHUB_MCP_CMD=npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-github

# ── LLM Routing ─────────────────────────────────
# Primary: Coder AI Gateway (centrally managed, no keys in workspaces)
AI_GATEWAY_URL=https://coder.your-org.com/ai-gateway
# Fallback: LiteLLM proxy (for self-hosted or air-gapped providers)
# LITELLM_PROXY_URL=http://litellm:4000
# LITELLM_API_KEY=sk-litellm-key

# ── GitHub identity ──────────────────────────────
# In Coder mode, GitHub identity is handled by Coder external auth.
# Configure a GitHub external auth provider in your Coder deployment and
# include the git-config module in your workspace templates. Each agent
# then acts under its logged-in Coder user's GitHub identity - git commits,
# the GitHub MCP server, and PRs are all auditable to that identity.

# ── Debug / Logging ─────────────────────────────
RUST_LOG=info,agent_team=debug,pocketflow_core=debug</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">GitHub identity comes from Coder, not pasted tokens</p>
        <div class="calloutText">Because Coder already authenticates each workspace owner, OpenFlows does not manage GitHub credentials itself. Configure a <strong>GitHub</strong> external auth provider in your Coder deployment and include the <code>git-config</code> module in your workspace templates. Every agent then acts under its own logged-in Coder user's GitHub identity - git commits, the GitHub MCP server, and PRs are all auditable to that identity. Nothing to rotate in <code>.env</code>.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Step 3.1: Model Registry -->
  <h3 id="model-registry">3.1 Model Registry (registry.json)</h3>
  <p>
    The registry tells each agent which LLM to use via the Coder AI Gateway. The
    <code>workspace_provider</code> field tells OpenFlows to provision ephemeral Coder
    workspaces instead of local worktrees. You can mix providers - FORGE on Claude Sonnet,
    SENTINEL on GPT-4o, NEXUS on Gemini - creating natural adversarial review.
  </p>
  <div class="codeBlock">
    <div class="codeHeader">
      <span class="codeLang">json</span>
    </div>
    <pre><code>{registryJson}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>
    The TUI wizard generates this for you. For manual setup, place <code>registry.json</code>
    next to your <code>.env</code> file. Hot-reloaded on every NEXUS poll - no restart required.
    The <code>coder_module</code> field names the Coder Registry module that installs the CLI
    code agent into each workspace. It must be published in your Coder deployment. Different
    agents can use different modules - for example, FORGE on <code>claude-code</code> while
    LORE uses <code>aider</code> - so you can match each agent's role to the best CLI for the job.
  </p>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">AI Gateway vs LiteLLM</p>
        <div class="calloutText">The Coder AI Gateway is the primary LLM route: API keys are managed centrally and never exposed inside workspaces. If you run air-gapped or need a self-hosted provider, configure LiteLLM as the fallback proxy. OpenFlows automatically falls back when the Gateway is unavailable.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Step 4: Verify -->
  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="verify">
    <span class="stepNumber">4</span>
    Verify Installation
  </h2>
  <p>Run the built-in doctor to check every dependency - including Coder connectivity - before starting the team.</p>
  <div class="codeBlock">
    <div class="codeHeader">
      <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
    </div>
    <pre><code>openflows-doctor</code></pre>
  </div>
  <p>Expected output:</p>
  <div class="codeBlock">
    <div class="codeHeader">
      <span class="codeLang">text</span>
    </div>
    <pre><code>$ openflows-doctor
  Coder deployment:  reachable (https://coder.your-org.com)
  Coder token:        valid (workspaces:create)
  Rust toolchain:     1.88.0
  Node.js:            v20.11.0
  Code agent CLI:     claude-code (Coder Registry module)
  Git:                2.44.0
  GitHub identity:     via Coder external auth
  AI Gateway:         reachable
  GITHUB_REPOSITORY:  owner/repo
  registry.json:      valid (workspace_provider: coder)</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="callout tip">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">Missing something?</p>
        <div class="calloutText">The doctor prints exactly which Coder endpoint, external-auth provider, or binary is missing and a link to where to get it. If it reports GitHub identity unresolved, confirm your Coder deployment has a GitHub external auth provider configured. Fix it, re-run, and proceed.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Step 5: Run -->
  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="run">
    <span class="stepNumber">5</span>
    Run the Autonomous Team
  </h2>
  <p>Two terminals: one runs the orchestration engine, the other shows real-time worker status.</p>

  <div class="codeBlock">
    <div class="codeHeader">
      <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
    </div>
    <pre><code># Terminal 1 - start the engine
openflows

# Terminal 2 - real-time dashboard
openflows-dashboard</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p>What happens when you start <code>openflows</code> - the ephemeral workspace lifecycle:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>NEXUS</strong> polls GitHub for open issues and worker state</li>
    <li>Idle issues are assigned to <strong>FORGE</strong> workers with unique ticket IDs</li>
    <li>NEXUS provisions an <strong>ephemeral Coder workspace</strong> for each FORGE worker</li>
    <li>FORGE runs inside the workspace, writes PLAN.md, and implements segment by segment</li>
    <li><strong>SENTINEL</strong> reviews every segment against 5 criteria before approving</li>
    <li><strong>VESSEL</strong> handles CI status, conflicts, and squash-merges approved PRs</li>
    <li><strong>LORE</strong> writes ADRs and updates CHANGELOG.md after each merge</li>
    <li>The Coder workspace is <strong>torn down</strong> after merge - no lingering state</li>
  </ol>

  <!-- Step 6: Logs -->
  <h2 class="stepHeading" id="logs">
    <span class="stepNumber">6</span>
    What the Logs Look Like
  </h2>
  <p>From open issue to merged PR. No human intervention. Each task runs in its own ephemeral Coder workspace.</p>
  <div class="codeBlock">
    <div class="codeHeader">
      <span class="codeLang">bash</span>
    </div>
    <pre><code>INFO  Starting REAL End-to-End Orchestration
INFO  Loaded 6 worker slots: [nexus, forge-1, forge-2, sentinel, vessel, lore]
INFO  Coder deployment reachable: https://coder.your-org.com
INFO  Found 3 open issues
INFO  Assigning issue #1 to forge-1  ->  T-001
INFO  Provisioning Coder workspace: forge-1/T-001
INFO  Workspace ready: forge-1/T-001 (template: openflows_agent)
INFO  Spawning code agent for forge-1 inside workspace
INFO  PLAN.md written - spawning SENTINEL for review
INFO  Contract AGREED - implementation starting
INFO  Worker forge-1 completed - PR opened: #1
INFO  CI status: success for PR #1
INFO  PR #1 merged successfully
INFO  LORE: ADR-001 written and committed
INFO  Tearing down Coder workspace: forge-1/T-001</code></pre>
  </div>

  <!-- Next Steps -->
  <h2 id="next-steps">Next Steps</h2>
  <p>Now that the team is running with ephemeral Coder workspaces, keep going:</p>
  <div class="docCards">
    {NEXT_STEPS.map(({ icon, title, desc, href }) => (
      <a key={href} href={href} class="docCard">
        <div class="docCardIcon">{icon}</div>
        <div class="docCardTitle">{title}</div>
        <div class="docCardDesc">{desc}</div>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="callout info">
    <div class="calloutBody">
      <div class="calloutContent">
        <p class="calloutTitle">Need help?</p>
        <div class="calloutText">If the doctor reports a missing dependency, Coder connectivity issue, or NEXUS fails to provision workspaces, start with the <a href="/docs/faq">FAQ</a> or open a discussion on <a href="https://github.com/The-AgenticFlow/OpenFlows/discussions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Discussions</a>.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="feedbackFooter">
    <p>Was this guide helpful? <a href="/docs/faq">Report an issue</a></p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
